support_evidence.1:
JSON object keys and string values use double quotes.
```javascript
const text = '{"city":"Oslo"}';
const place = JSON.parse(text);
console.log(place.city);
```
This prints Oslo.

support_evidence.2:
Parsing can succeed without giving you the shape you need.
```javascript
console.log(JSON.parse("null"));
console.log(JSON.parse("[2, 5]"));
```
The first value is null; the second is an array, not a city record.

support_evidence.4:
A guard can reject those cases before reading a property.
```javascript
function hasCity(value) {
  if (value === null || typeof value !== "object" || Array.isArray(value)) return false;
  return typeof value.city === "string";
}
```

support_evidence.5:
Malformed JSON throws an error. A catch block can handle it instead of stopping the program.
```javascript
try {
  JSON.parse("not JSON");
} catch (error) {
  console.log("Could not read the text");
}
```

support_evidence.2:
Assignment can update an outer let binding.
```javascript
let mood = "quiet";
if (true) {
  mood = "curious";
}
console.log(mood);
```
This prints curious.

support_evidence.3:
A new declaration with the same name creates a separate inner binding. This is called shadowing.
```javascript
let mood = "quiet";
if (true) {
  let mood = "curious";
  console.log(mood);
}
console.log(mood);
```
The inner log prints curious; the outer log prints quiet.

support_evidence.2:
The || operator chooses the right value when the left value is falsy.
```javascript
console.log(0 || 10);
console.log("" || "Guest");
```
These display 10 and Guest.

support_evidence.3:
The ?? operator chooses the right value only for null or undefined.
```javascript
console.log(0 ?? 10);
console.log(false ?? true);
```
These preserve 0 and false.

support_evidence.2:
Use JSON when storing a small record.
```javascript
const encoded = JSON.stringify({ color: "teal" });
console.log(encoded);
```
This produces text that can later be parsed.

This example subtracts one at a time.
```javascript
let remaining = 3;
let turns = 0;
while (remaining > 0) {
  remaining = remaining - 1;
  turns = turns + 1;
}
console.log(turns);
```

support_evidence.1:
The same function can be called with another receiver.
```javascript
function read() { return this.name; }
const first = { name: "Pine", read };
const second = { name: "Oak", read };
console.log(first.read(), second.read());
```
This prints Pine Oak.

support_evidence.3:
bind creates a function with a chosen receiver.
```javascript
const card = { name: "Pine", read() { return this.name; } };
const readLater = card.read.bind(card);
console.log(readLater());
```

support_evidence.1:
Object.create(parent) makes an object with parent as its prototype.
```javascript
const defaults = { color: "teal" };
const badge = Object.create(defaults);
console.log(badge.color);
```
The lookup finds color on defaults.

support_evidence.3:
Writing a normal own data property can shadow an inherited one.
```javascript
badge.color = "gold";
console.log(badge.color, defaults.color);
```
These read gold and teal.

[0-9] matches one ASCII digit. {3} repeats the preceding pattern exactly three times.
```javascript
const code = /^BOX-[0-9]{3}$/;
console.log(code.test("BOX-123"));
```

support_evidence.1:
A fulfilled Promise does not make its then callback run immediately inside the current call stack.
```javascript
console.log("A");
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("B"));
console.log("C");
```
This logs A, C, B.

support_evidence.2:
yield pauses the generator after producing a value.
```javascript
function* colors() {
  yield "teal";
  yield "gold";
}
const it = colors();
console.log(it.next());
```
The first result has value teal and done false.

A class selector has higher specificity than an element selector.
```css
.label { color: teal; }
p { color: purple; }
```
A paragraph with class="label" is teal even though the p rule comes later.

support_evidence.3:
When matching rules have equal specificity, the later declaration wins.
```css
.label { color: teal; }
.label { color: brown; }
```
The second rule wins because these selectors have equal specificity.

support_evidence.4:
An inherited value comes from a parent. A child’s own declared color takes precedence over an inherited color.
```css
body { color: navy; }
p { color: purple; }
```
The paragraph is purple; content without its own color can inherit navy.

support_evidence.3:
An outline can mark focus without changing the element’s box size.
```css
input:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid purple;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```

support_evidence.4:
overflow: auto provides scrolling when content does not fit.
```css
.window { width: 160px; overflow: auto; }
.line { width: 320px; }
```

support_evidence.1:
For normal author declarations, a later layer has higher precedence.
```css
@layer reset, theme;
@layer reset { #message { color: purple; } }
@layer theme { p { color: teal; } }
```
The theme rule wins for the matching paragraph despite the earlier rule’s ID selector.
